{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"### The strategy\n\nWe can first rewrite each rational term of $g$ as a negative power of $x$.\n\nThen, the derivatives of these terms can be found using **the power rule**:\n\n$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^n)=n\\cdot x^{n-1}$\n\n(Remember that this applies even when $n$ is negative.)","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"### Rewriting rational terms as negative powers\n\n$\\begin{align}\ng(x)\u0026=\\dfrac{1}{x^4}-\\dfrac{3}{x^2}+x^3\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=x^{-4}-3x^{-2}+x^3\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"### Differentiating using the power rule\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}g'(x)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-4}-3x^{-2}+x^3)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-4})-3\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-2})+\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^3)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-4x^{-5}-3(-2)x^{-3}+3x^2\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-\\dfrac{4}{x^5}+\\dfrac{6}{x^3}+3x^2\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $g'(x)=-\\dfrac{4}{x^5}+\\dfrac{6}{x^3}+3x^2$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"Let $g(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^4}-\\dfrac{3}{x^2}+x^3$.\n\n$g'(x)=$ [[☃ expression 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"considered":"correct","form":false,"key":0,"simplify":false,"value":"-\\frac{4}{x^5}+\\frac{6}{x^3}+3x^2"}],"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
